course: dictionary
lesson: 6237
title: Mock: laugh at, pretend, practice
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. «Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.» Here mock means laughing at someone in an unkind way.
explain: Now read «The children had a mock battle in the playground.» A battle is a fight, so a mock battle is a pretend fight. Mock here means not real, made to look like the real thing.
explain: In the UK, a mock is a practice exam before the real one. «Our mock exams help us get ready for the real tests.» Ask yourself: what word fits here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Mock can be a verb, an adjective or a noun.
fact: To mock someone means to laugh at them, or copy them, in an unkind way to make them look silly.
fact: As an adjective, mock means not real, like «mock exam» or «mock trial».
fact: In the UK, «mocks» are practice exams before the real ones.
fact: If others mock someone's religion, festival or clothes, tell them to stop and tell a teacher.
exercise: «Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.» What does mocking mean here? | *Laughing at someone in an unkind way | Singing a happy song | Helping someone choose their clothes | Practising for a test
exercise: «The children had a mock battle in the playground.» What does mock mean here? | *Pretend, not real | Very loud | A real fight with a winner | Tidy and clean
exercise: «Our mock exams help us get ready for the real tests.» In the UK, what is a mock exam? | *A practice exam before the real one | A test that nobody marks | A prize for working hard | The final exam of the year
test: «If others mock someone's festival, tell them to stop and tell a teacher.» What does mock mean in this sentence? | To make a pretend cake | To write a long story | *To laugh at someone in an unkind way | To cook a special meal
test: Which phrase means a pretend court case? | a loud trial | a silent trial | a trial of speed | *a mock trial
exam: «Making or sharing fake pictures of real people to mock them is bullying and can be against the law.» What does mock mean here? | To copy someone's handwriting kindly | To make a model of a house | *To laugh at people in an unkind way | To practise for a test
